00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:02.629 align:start position:0%
 
help everyone  i am glad to present our project data  driven survey to map the relation  between oral microbiome and  neuropsychiatry disease  i'm dr priya from hyderabad this poster  depicts my whole project which includes  introduction  methods use results the data  visualization and analysis and  conclusion  i'll start off by explaining why this  project idea we choose in specific  so human body is colonized with  trillions of microorganism in various  parts of the body  like throat cut skin etc  ratio between the species can be totally  different from person to person  each individual microbiome is unique  like your fingerprint or bite mark  human and their microbiota have a mutual  relationship which benefit each other  but they create problems if the balance  between the colonies is gone  in a healthy adult body there are  approximately 10 times more bacteria  than human cells  the microbial community in our body  include members of hugely diverse array  of kingdoms and species  when does the microbiome enter into our  body  the uterus is totally sterile but as  soon as you're exposed  when you're born to millions of microbes  that immediately colonize your body  babies which are born vaginally are  covered with microbes living in their  mother's birth canal while babies born  by c-section are exposed to microbes on  skin  even where the baby is born and what it  is fed  will  affect the microbe composition  as they grow up they pick microbe from  the environment also  as an adult lot of things influence your  microbiome composition  like food you eat the illness you get  medications your prescribed the  workplace etc  oral microbiome is a community of  microbes that live within the oral  cavity locations like palatal region  tonsiller area throat  tongue  gingiva  teeth and supreme subgingival plaque  attached to the teeth etc so when  imbalance occur due to various  conditions like poor oral health or  serostomia which is dry mouth which  leads to dysbiosis which in turn can  release various metabolites and  inflammatory protein which leads to  inflammation which in turn leads to  changes in the brain function so from  the oral cavity  various pathways are available to reach  the brain like vascular system through  the blood brain barrier the olfactory  tract the perivascular space of brain  and the circumvention organs the  microbiome from the oral cavity enter  the gut and cause further changes in the  industrial layer as seen in the picture  during dysbiosis the tight junction get  loosened thinning of the mucosal layer  happen the cell count get depleted which  pairs the pathway for microbes to enter  the circulatory system which in turn  affects the brain health and individual  health there are various pathways how  microbes interact with the brain  first through the immune cells second  through metabolites and  neurotransmitters like serotonin  and the main way is through entry  nervous system  through the central nervous system by  the vagus nerve so the neuroactive  molecules from the gut  get transferred to the brain through the  vagus nerve directly  so in our project we are trying to  figure out how specific species of  microbes are affecting the brain and  causing neuropsychiatric symptoms and  how we can tailor the microbiome to  maximize mental health benefits  so here we are focusing on six major  neuropsychiatry disease like alzheimer's  autism schizophrenia major depressive  disorder adhd and bipolar disorder  neuropsychiatry disease is the third  most common  for disability adjusted life years  globally and  there is a growing concern about the  wave of neuropsychiatric burden during  this scuba 19 pandemic  some common neuropsychiatric  manifestation include delirium mild  cognitive impairment mood swing insomnia  and psychosis  so for this project we use this biome  data set to estimate the association  between oral microbiome attributable to  neuropsychiatric disorders  this database covers microbial  composition changes in different kinds  of disease  so the dataset include disease name  sample type post type microorganism type  methods used and the qualitative outcome  these are the keywords which were used  this table highlights how much data we  got for each disease and the number of  samples  which was involved in each disease  then we use  complex graph analytics  we try to answer the following question  using graph theory  so which organism is more influence in  each particular disease and all six  diseases together which organism is  least influence in each disease and all  disease together  which disease is influenced by microbes  less and more  from the complex graph we calculated  various indicators using mathematical  formulas like between a centrality  centrality of proximity eagan vector  centrality so this is one of the graph  produced from the data set  these are few screenshots of the results  which we got from our analysis the major  key findings are  autism spectrum disorder is influenced  by more microorganism bipolar is least  influenced by microorganism  lactobacillus species and saturated  species have influenced more disease in  this network  then way cellar species gaming or  species and pseudo monodacia species has  influenced less disease in this network  so we tried to got all the answers  through this graph analytics theory  the future direction of a project is by  doing further downstream analysis we can  understand the specific metabolites  genes  coenzymes involved in that particular  disease and can treat effectively so  hereby i conclude there is a huge  benefit of microbiome in precision  medicine  the ease of manipulation and delivery of  therapeutic aimed at modulating  microbiome function can be very  effective  the adoption of routine sampling  associated with wellness center visit to  primary care physician in combination  with human genome and other clinical  data which will enhance the decision  process around the treatment option very  effectively  these are the references used for our  project  thank you for this great opportunity

